        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $x=0,1,2,3,4$, respectively: $y=7,\\;6\\frac12,\\;6,\\;5\\frac12,\\;5$.",
          "worked": "For each increase of $1$ in $x$, $y$ decreases by $\\frac12$. Starting from $(2,6)$, work backwards and forwards:\n$$\\begin{align*}y(0)&=6+2\\times\\frac12=7\\\\y(1)&=6+\\frac12=6\\frac12\\\\y(2)&=6\\\\y(3)&=6-\\frac12=5\\frac12\\\\y(4)&=6-2\\times\\frac12=5\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0,
        "responseSpace": "scaffold"
      },
      {
        "id": "p2-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the equation of the line.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=-\\frac12x+7$",
          "worked": "The table gives the $y$-intercept $7$. Using $y=mx+c$:\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=-\\frac12x+7\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
